| **№130 THE FIFO BUG CLASS IS NOW STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE (History; user-directed hardening):** | **USER: "AI chats don't always listen to the docs... this FIFO swap could happen again, except on a bigger scale once every island and actors in the Great Sea is here. Multiple variables, all at the push of a button." CORRECT, and documentation was the wrong fix.** Evidence it is systemic, not a discipline problem: this bug family has now bitten **three times** - No.64 (orphan entry stolen by the next HENNA0 create), No.129 (two ported actors leaking), and the near-miss below. Blast radius scales with cast size: one leak on a populated Great Sea shifts hundreds of actors, silently, with correct dialogue masking it. **ROOT OF THE CLASS: the queue was ORDER-DEPENDENT.** `takePendingSpawn` falls back to popping the FIFO **front**, so any actor that fails to consume hands its entry to the NEXT actor. Every actor author had to remember a rule whose violation is invisible and global. **FIX (structural, one place):** every push now returns a **token**; after `fopAcM_create` the spawner calls `dExtNpcMount_reapPendingSpawn(seq, id)`. If the entry is still queued, it is **moved out of the FIFO and re-keyed to the owning actor id**. Order-dependence is gone: a late or forgetful actor can neither be served another's entry nor cause another to be served its own. Wired at BOTH spawn sites (population + BG mount). **NEAR-MISS I CAUGHT BEFORE SHIPPING (worth recording):** my first cut simply ERASED the unconsumed entry. That is right for a synchronous actor that forgot and **catastrophic for a phase-based actor that consumes on a later frame** - `fopAcM_create` may defer create (exactly why the push happens first, No.45), so erasing would have deleted the entry out from under my own bridge actor and re-broken the cast. Re-keying is safe for both. **I nearly shipped a fourth instance of the very bug I was hardening against.** **ALSO REMOVED:** the old failure path popped the FIFO **fr